<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/hero-coastal-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hero-coastal-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hero-coastal-house.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="747" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span><a href="/claim-valuation-services/">Services</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span>Homeowner Claim Valuation</nav><p class="tag">Service</p><h1>Homeowner Insurance Appraisal for Rhode Island Houses</h1><p class="lede">Triple-deckers, capes, colonials and shingle-style cottages each fail differently in a storm. We value your home’s loss from the ground up.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn glass" href="/request-a-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Property</dt><dd>1–4 family homes, condos</dd></div><div><dt>Pricing</dt><dd>Local, line-item</dd></div><div><dt>Start</dt><dd>Free claim review</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Homeowner insurance appraisal in Rhode Island looks different from appraisal in newer, single-style markets. Much of the housing stock here was built before 1950: three-decker multi-family homes in Pawtucket, Woonsocket and Providence’s West End, Victorian and Colonial Revival houses on the East Side, mid-century capes in Warwick and Cranston, and summer cottages along the South County shore. Each has construction details that a quick desk estimate can miss.</p>
<h2>Where homeowner estimates usually fall short</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> A slope or room is left off, or only the visibly damaged area is priced when the repair can’t stop there.</li>
<li><strong>Materials.</strong> Plaster walls priced as drywall, old-growth trim priced as stock, or cedar shingles priced as vinyl.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Siding, roofing or flooring that can no longer be matched, so a patch would leave a visible mismatch.</li>
<li><strong>Access and labor.</strong> Steep roofs, third-floor units, tight urban lots and staging that real contractors charge for.</li>
<li><strong>Code and permit items.</strong> Work your city or town building official will require once repairs begin.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Multi-family and owner-occupied rentals</h2>
<p>Many Rhode Island owners live in one unit of a two- or three-family home and rent the others. That can bring loss-of-rents coverage, tenant contents and separate meters and systems into the claim. We value the building loss and help you organize what belongs to which coverage.</p>
<h2>Condo unit owners</h2>
<p>If you own a condo, the association’s master policy and your own unit-owner policy may both be involved. We read the condo declarations alongside both policies so the damage is valued under the right one.</p>
<h3>What to send us first</h3>
<ul><li>Your declarations page and full policy</li><li>The carrier’s complete estimate</li><li>Photos from the day of the loss onward</li><li>Any contractor estimate you’ve received</li></ul><h2>Questions about homeowner claim valuation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you handle three-decker and multi-family homes?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Two- and three-family homes are a large share of Rhode Island’s older housing, and we value them regularly, including common stairs, porches and shared roofs.</p></div></details><details><summary>My house has plaster walls. Does that matter?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It can. Plaster and lath repairs cost more than drywall and take different labor. If the carrier priced drywall, that difference belongs in the discussion.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you help if my claim is still open?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Many owners call before appraisal is invoked. An estimate review often shows whether a supplement or appraisal makes more sense.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="related"><a href="/claim-valuation-services/commercial-condo-hoa-claims/"><small>Service</small><span>Commercial, Condo &amp; HOA Claims</span><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" str